{
  "Thousands of resources and appointments: the scheduler builds only what is in view, and lays out only what changed.": "",
  "1,000 resources, 10,000 appointments": "",
  "A timeline week with a row for each of 1,000 lines and 10,000 tasks over them (generated). Scroll down and sideways: the rows, cells and tasks in view are built as they come. The line under it says how long the first show took and how many elements the scheduler holds.": "",
  "200 groups in a week": "",
  "A week grouped by 200 lines: 1,400 columns of 48 cells and 4,000 tasks. Only the columns in view (and a screen on each side) are built.": "",
  "How it stays fast": "",
  "Through `Virtualization`: only the rows in view (and a few on each side) are built. Each row is as tall as its lanes; the model works the height out (lanes × lane height), the list reads it. While an appointment is dragged its rows grow in place and the list is given the heights after.": "",
  "A window kept by the model (`setWindow`): the cells and appointments of the part in view and a screen on each side. Scrolling moves the window on by a few cells at a time; the rows show it through a keyed `.map` over the window’s slice.": "",
  "The same window over the columns: with many groups only the columns in view are built, each at its own place.": "",
  "Each row of a timeline is laid out on its own; a drag lays out only the rows it was and is in. The appointments are read once for every layout until they change.": "",
  "The range shown is what is asked for (`rangeFilter`), with the resources shown when grouped by a field of one value.": "",
  "Every row and cell built (`'standard'`), or only what is in view (`'virtual'`); `'auto'`: virtual past about 2,000 cells (rows × cells along the time, columns × time rows). A view’s own wins.": ""
}
